-## Widescreen and native resolution on a phone (bring-up plan, current)
+## Widescreen and native resolution on a phone (route proven, current)
 
-The panel in question is 2868x1320 (19.5:9 = 2.17:1, wider than 16:9). The host now supports wide picture shapes under `DM_SCREEN_ASPECT` (1.3 to 2.4): a wide shape takes output 1920x880 at 2.17 (height a multiple of 8; the 48 grid left the picture 2.2 % off the screen and the present cropped), keeps the 16:9 vertical angle in view (`k` floored at 1) and presents the picture to the whole canvas. But `screen_aspect` is off in this profile and the host only follows the shape where the build has screen-shape stubs (`DM_GAME_SCREEN_ASPECT` - today only Breath of the Wild 1.9.0 and Animal Crossing 3.0.3). FFT needs its own stubs, keyed to `ace351a1…`.
+The panel in question is 2868x1320 (19.5:9 ≈ 2.17:1, wider than 16:9). The route is the game's own render pair, not host reshaping, and not a forced size past its load (the earlier Build B reading is superseded: its crash was the 16 MiB swapchain-pool ceiling below, not the patching itself):
 
-What is known about 1.5.1's `main` (`ace351a145c07a51f8e239666640a0d8`):
+1. **The render pair.** 1280x720 is written by two places that must agree: `main+0x17910` (the display settings the renderer is handed; its +0x18 and +0x30 hold the 64-bit pair, width low and height high) and `main+0x68c` (the vtable override at `.data 0x808268` slot +0x20 of the same fields). From the struct the crop (`main+0x3bd2c`), the swap-chain textures (`main+0x4fed0` -> `+0x51220` -> `+0x3b770` -> `nvnWindowBuilderSetTextures`) and the viewport are all built, and the binary holds no 16:9 value (no f32 1.7777778, no f64 16/9, no u16/u32 1280,720 pair anywhere in text, rodata or data), so the projection computes from the size at run time. On iOS the host presents the swap chain at its own aspect, so the pair chooses both resolution and shape.
+2. **Two pool gates** - every size past them died at `nvnMemoryPoolMap(NULL)` before these were found, and they are why the earlier forced-size builds crashed: `main+0xee9c`, the game's nn graphics pool reservation, 1280 -> 1792 MiB (the game drains the shared GPU pool during boot; the host builds the named pool in lazy chunks, so nothing commits up front), and `main+0xf1c4`, the game's own swapchain pool, 16 -> 32 MiB: its segment allocator (`main+0xad90`) cannot serve a 3-texture swapchain past 16 MiB, and the failed allocation is the null-pool crash. This was the missing piece in every earlier height-above-720 crash, 1920x882 included.
+3. **The wide-viewport cut.** The render-target/viewport builder (1.5.1: `main+0x8ecac`, size 0x238, cut at +0x6c/+0x78; 1.4.0: the same function at `main+0x7572c`) clamps any target wider than 16:9 to a 16:9 window of full height, centered (a pillarbox), and the clamped width becomes the projection aspect (`camera+0xcd8` from w25/w23). Two nops drop the clamp, keeping the full width, so the render target, aspect and UI offsets follow the picture; targets at or below 16:9 take the original path.
 
-- **A forced native render pair is a dead end.** A build that wrote 1920x1080 render sizes past the game's own load (Build B) crashed at `h_nvnMemoryPoolMap` (`x0 = 0`, pool 1792 MB); `DM_ALIAS_SIZE=0xC8000000` grew the alias to 6.9 GB and the crash was byte-identical. Civilization VII's bring-up ([seedds/nxnative issue #3033](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/3033)) saw the same: forced sizes downstream of the size load revert or null-look-up; the working cut redirected the handheld size **load** to the docked literal instead. FFT renders 720p handheld even with `DM_OPMODE=1`, so the render-pair load site is the gate.
-- 1.5.1 `main` holds 1920x1080 literals at file offsets `0x38E38` and `0x3181F8` (the docked pair a redirect can reuse).
-- The probe route is the one [Civilization VII's bring-up](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/3033) took: find the function that copies the handheld render sizes (a 1280x720 literal, its reader writes a display-profile struct), cut its load to the `0x38E38`/`0x3181F8` literals, then check the log's swapchain size and `WindowCrop` before any other stub.
-- After 1080p boots: widen the picture with `screen_aspect on` and the projection stubs (the engine's perspective projection takes the view ratio; hunt the frame's four projection inputs as [Ocarina of Time's widescreen](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/3037) did), then the HUD layout anchors, then touch scale. Movies stay 16:9.
+On 1.4.0 (`3cfd4578...`) this route ran on device at 1568x720 (2.178:1) with projection, FOV, HUD and touch following the picture. On 1.5.1 (`ace351a1...`) the 16:9 variant - the same four size sites at 1920x1080 (the binary also holds that literal at `0x38E38`/`0x3181F8`, the docked pair, though this route needs no redirect: the size writers are patched in place), both pools and the wake cut - sat at 59.9 fps across a whole session at crisp 1080p. All ten cuts are registered for `ace351a1...` in [patches.py](patches.py), the pair as **1920x888** (the 8-multiple height the 19.5:9 panel's shape takes at 1920 wide; the 16:9 1080p fallback swaps the two height encodings to `movk x8,#0x438,lsl#32`). `screen_aspect` stays off in this profile: the game's own projection computes from the pair and the host presents the wide swap chain directly - the switch only applies where a build has screen-shape stubs.
+
+Open follow-ups, in order: the movies (the game letterboxes its own 16:9 movies; check they center on the wide picture), the touch layout at the taller picture (it followed the pair at 720 height on 1.4.0), and the messy textures in cutscenes and battle (see Open below).
 
 ## Open
 
-- No source gives an iPhone measurement (frame rate, footprint); development.md says only "playable on an iPhone".
-- No screen-shape (widescreen) stubs yet; the plan above starts from the render-pair load site on a 1.5.1 dump.
+- **Messy textures in cutscenes and battle** (newspaper-print noise over the picture on an iPhone 18 Pro Max, 1080p 16:9 build). The prime leads, in order: upstream [issue #2275](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/2275)'s `sampler ID 768 not registered` (one line per session in this game's device logs too, once read as benign - it drives every third frame of the CRI Mana movies and in-engine sprites to horizontal streaks, and a host fix for reading such samplers, SeparateSamplerId raw IDs from uniform-buffer words, landed 2026-11-12 and is in this engine); the 19 unbounded shader keys of `c1_full` from the update's shader build (bindings whose slot was not decoded); the 30 texture/cover pixel pairs of the DmIOS shader audit. All three sit in the project's handoff evidence; a device log of a messy-textures session with `DM_NVN_TEXINFO=1280x720` is the next probe.
+- `shader_versions` stays `v393216` as registered; if a build from a 1.5.1-only supplies differs in the technique files, add `v524288` there and rebuild the shader chain.
+- No iPhone fps/footprint source beyond development.md's "playable on an iPhone"; the device sessions above are the numbers.
